@@ -639,6 +639,37 @@
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args)
     # New caller; hookimpls will get new_arg="get this".
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args, new_arg="get this")
+
+.. _hookimpl_arg_default:
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* may also declare a default value for an argument. The value a
+hookimpl receives for such an argument is determined in this order:
+
+1. the value passed by the hook call,
+2. the default value declared by the hookspec,
+3. the default value declared by the hookimpl itself.
+
+The hookimpl's own default is therefore only used when neither the caller nor
+the hookspec provides the argument, for example when a new hookimpl runs
+against an older hookspec which doesn't have the argument yet:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, args, new_arg="impl default"):
+        pass
+
+
+    # The hookimpl gets new_arg="get this".
+    pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args, new_arg="get this")
+
+This applies to plain hookimpls, wrappers and old-style hook wrappers alike.
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7
+    Previously, a hookimpl's default value was always used, even if the hook
+    call passed an explicit value for the argument.
 
 
